  "slug": "machine-learning-cae6",
  "summary": "Prospective and multi-method benchmarks now test whether ML-guided design and structure prediction hold up beyond retrospective metrics.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is systematic evaluation of machine-learning models for structure-based molecular design, spanning antibodies, cyclic peptides, aptamers, and small molecules. Several items move beyond retrospective scoring. A blinded, prospective benchmark of 511 AI-designed antibodies tests affinity maturation, HCDR3 affinity ranking, and CDR design against experimental affinity and developability data. A 412-complex antibody-antigen co-folding benchmark finds that ten methods show recent gains, mainly in CDRH3 modeling. For cyclic peptides, Boltz and Protenix generate strong poses, but ranking is the bottleneck, which a rescoring model improves. New tools include NACraft for all-atom aptamer design through structure-model feedback, AFilter for interface-energy filtering of AlphaFold3 epitope models, and cdrGPT for CDRH3 generation. Benchmarks of 12 generative methods across 176 systems and fixed-budget virtual screening comparisons extend the same evaluative emphasis to small molecules.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-08-25"
}
